#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Mnemosyne self-application pre-commit gate — runs on every `git commit`.
#
# Mnemosyne dogfoods its own validator against the repo's atomic store
# (the SSOT) + code citation defense before every commit. Plus a clippy
# gate when `.rs` files are staged (workspace lint baseline).
#
# Gates:
#
#   2. Code citation defense — `mnemosyne-cli validate-code-refs`
#      always runs; subcommand silently skips when
#      `[plugins.set_equality_validator]` is unconfigured (5-min setup
#      promise). Otherwise scans `paths` for `<entry_id_prefix><digits>`
#      and `§<id>` citations and rejects on hallucination / unbound /
#      decay / inventory / symbol-mismatch violations (severity per config).
#
#   3. Workspace gate — `mnemosyne-cli validate-workspace` when the atomic
#      sidecar (`docs/.atomic/workspace.atomic.json`, the SSOT) is staged.
#      Store-direct: T1 prose-orphan delta, T2 frozen-ledger, T3/T4 style,
#      publishable/audit divergence, ledger match, commit↔ledger drift.
#
#   4. Format gate — `cargo fmt --all --check` when any `.rs` file is
#      staged. rustfmt 4-space repo-wide (rustfmt.toml, edition 2021);
#      rejects unformatted code so layout stays uniform.
#
#   5. Clippy gate — `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`
#      when any `.rs` file is staged. R308 D9 closure (warnings → deny
#      baseline): any new clippy warning trips the gate. Workspace-level
#      allow list in root Cargo.toml covers `doc_lazy_continuation` (rustdoc
#      renders both forms) and `inconsistent_digit_grouping` (YYYY_MM_DD
#      date u64 literals). Per-site `#[allow]` for API-shape exemptions
#      (`result_large_err` on tonic interceptors; `too_many_arguments` on
#      the `scan_paths_no_resolvers` test helper).
#
#   5a. Item-citation gate (R1077/R1078) — `tools/item-citations` when any
#      `.rs` is staged: every `[`name`]` in this workspace's code names an
#      item rustdoc can resolve, over every target `cargo metadata` lists
#      rather than the 25 of 136 `cargo doc --workspace` reaches.
#
#   ⇒ THE FOUR `.rs` GATES BELOW (5c, 5d, 5f, 5g) RUN OVER THE WORKSPACES THIS
#      COMMIT'S OWN FILES LIVE IN (R1292), one run each, resolved by walking up
#      from every staged `.rs` to the first `[workspace]` above it. They used to
#      fire on ANY staged `.rs` and walk the ROOT, which this repository has
#      twenty-six workspace roots' worth of reasons not to do: staging a file
#      under `tools/<crate>/` ran four whole-workspace walks over a tree that did
#      not contain it. Measured by it happening — R1291's new test spawned a
#      binary reading `CARGO` without naming it, `named-environment` RAN on that
#      commit over the root, and the hosted job found it a round later.
#
#   5c. Blind-wait gate (R1081) — `tools/blind-waits` when any `.rs` is
#      staged: in test code a wait ends on a CONDITION and the budget that
#      bounds it is NAMED. R1073's red main was `conflicts > 0` after 500ms
#      of eight writers — an assertion about the scheduler dressed as one
#      about the store — and the root workspace held four more of the shape,
#      including one whose too-short sleep PASSED an assertion of absence.
#      Parses with `syn`: no pattern can tell a synchronising `sleep` from
#      the retry step of a poll, and that difference is the whole law.
#
#   5b. Separate-workspace gate (R517) — fmt + clippy on the in-repo
#      crates that carry their OWN `[workspace]` (tools/experiment-harness,
#      bench/, studio/), which Gates 4/5 above never reach (`--all` /
#      `--workspace` only see root members). Structural, not hardcoded: for
#      each staged `.rs`, walk up to its owning `[workspace]` root and gate
#      any non-root one on its own `--manifest-path`, so a new `tools/<crate>`
#      is covered automatically. Runs ONLY when such a file is staged (the
#      root gate stays fast — the build-speed reason those crates are split
#      out). pre-push stays root-only by design: it validates the publishable
#      production workspace, while THIS gate keeps all committed Rust clean.
#      Closes the silent-fail where harness code committed unformatted because
#      no gate compiled it.
#
#   5f. Scratch-ownership gate (R1193) — `tools/unowned-scratch` when any
#      `.rs` is staged: a path built from `std::env::temp_dir()`, which every
#      process on the machine shares, names the process that owns it. R1175
#      repaired six such paths after two overlapping runs made one delete the
#      other's fixtures mid-write, and its carry said what was missing:
#      nothing stops the seventh one.
#
#   5e. Declaration gate (R1189) — `tools/unread-declaration`, on EVERY
#      commit rather than on a staged path: every top-level key in
#      `.claude/remote-build.toml` is one `bx` reports reading, asked of the
#      program (`--explain-declaration`) rather than of a copy of its schema.
#      The reader is machine-wide and outside this checkout, so the half that
#      rots moves no byte here — which is why there is no staged-file guard.
#      A repository declaring nothing is exit 0 with a sentence.
#
#   5h. Requirement gate (R1210) — `tools/undeclared-requirement`, on EVERY
#      commit: every package this repository's CI installs is one
#      `.claude/remote-build.toml` names. CI is the one far side this
#      repository specifies completely, so what it installs is the set a
#      bare machine is already known to lack; the build machine's copy of
#      that set had no witness, and `protobuf-compiler` was missing from it
#      for the two weeks between R888 repairing the runner and a build host
#      compiling 269 crates to find out.
#
#   5i. Anchor gate (R1291) — `tools/injection-harness anchors`, on EVERY
#      commit: which of this repository's own sweeps THIS CHANGE breaks. An
#      anchor is exact text and the source it names moves, so the round that
#      moves a gate holds the information and nothing asked it. The index is
#      held against `HEAD`, so a dead anchor somebody else left is printed and
#      never blocking — this tree has more than one writer — and a new one is
#      refused, with the two repairs named apart (`forget` when the injection's
#      SUBJECT went with the code, re-anchor and re-run when only the line did).
#
#   7. Second-machine gate (R1234) — `tools/one-machine --send`, on EVERY
#      commit: this tree's syscall census is put on a machine the placement
#      program chooses, detached, and `pre-push` reads what it wrote. The
#      census answers a question about a MACHINE, and every one of the four
#      defects this repository has found in it was found where its author was
#      not sitting; until this gate there was no automated second machine at
#      all. REPORTS AND NEVER BLOCKS, for the reason `pre-push`'s CI reporter
#      does not: a fleet that is busy is not this repository's defect.
#
# Install (one-time per clone):
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# Gated by `crates/mnemosyne-cli/tests/git_hooks_smoke.rs` (R899): every
# gate below has an accept/reject pair run against a throwaway repo wired
# to THIS file, so deleting a block turns a test red instead of going
# unnoticed. Add a case with any gate you add.
#
# Bypass: `--no-verify` (discouraged; the project policy treats audit
# gates as load-bearing, not nudges).

set -euo pipefail

repo_root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$repo_root"

# Gate 0 — the identity this commit would carry.
#
# FIRST, ahead of anything that costs: a commit that is going to be refused
# for its author should be refused before `scripts/mn` resolves a tool. It is
# also the one refusal here that a revert does not undo, because the remote is
# public and a push does not un-publish. lib/ident-gate.sh carries the
# incident, which is not hypothetical.
# Resolved from THIS HOOK's directory and never from `repo_root`. The two are
# the same in a working clone and different in the only place that grades this
# gate: `git_hooks_smoke` runs the real hook with a FIXTURE tree as its working
# directory, and a fixture has no `.githooks/lib`. `repo_root` was the first
# spelling and all three cases failed on a missing file -- which is the tests
# doing their job, and the reason the library belongs to the hook rather than
# to whatever tree the hook is pointed at.
# shellcheck source=.githooks/lib/ident-gate.sh
source "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/lib/ident-gate.sh"
ident_gate_pending "pre-commit" || exit 1

ATOMIC_SIDECAR="docs/.atomic/workspace.atomic.json"

# Round 823 — ONE resolver, `scripts/mn`, shared with pre-push and with
# interactive use. This hook used to prefer `target/release/mnemosyne-cli` if it
# existed and fall back to the PATH entry, and both could hand back the wrong
# tool: the release artifact was taken WITHOUT a freshness check, so an older
# build could gate a commit, and `~/.cargo/bin` is a slot shared with every other
# checkout on the machine. `scripts/mn` asks cargo instead, which is cheap now
# that the build script stops rerunning on every build.
cli=(scripts/mn)

# THE POPULATION EVERY GATE BELOW IS KEYED ON, AND IT REFUSES RATHER THAN
# ANSWERING EMPTY (R1281). This line ended in `|| true` until then, which
# disarmed `set -e` for exactly the case it should not have: `git diff --cached
# --name-only` returns non-zero only on ERROR — an empty index is a zero exit
# with no output — so the only thing that clause could ever mask was a read that
# failed. And a failed read here does not look like a failure: it looks like a
# repository with nothing staged, which every gate below reads as nothing to
# check, so the hook walks through Gates 3 to 6 and exits 0. A commit gate that
# accepts because it could not see is the shape this repository keeps paying for
# — `commit-msg`'s `2>/dev/null` was the same thing one hook over.
if ! staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR); then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the staged list could not be read"
        echo "  every gate below is keyed on it, and an unreadable list is not an empty one"
    } >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Gate 2 — code citation defense (R257+). Internally skips when
# [plugins.set_equality_validator] is unconfigured.
echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] code citation defense — validate-code-refs" >&2
if ! "${cli[@]}" validate-code-refs; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — code citation violations"
        echo "  fix: verify each cited entry_id / §<id> / inventory id exists,"
        echo "       or register stale citations in mnemosyne.toml [[orphan_ledger]]"
    } >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Gate 3 — workspace validate when the atomic sidecar (the SSOT) is staged.
if printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qx -- "$ATOMIC_SIDECAR"; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] atomic sidecar staged — validate-workspace" >&2
    "${cli[@]}" validate-workspace
fi

# Gate 4 — rustfmt on .rs changes. Enforces the 4-space repo-wide baseline
# (rustfmt.toml); rejects unformatted code so the layout stays uniform.
if printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qE '\.rs$'; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — cargo fmt --all --check" >&2
    if ! cargo fmt --all --check; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — unformatted code"
            echo "  fix: cargo fmt --all && git add -u"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi
fi

# ── WHAT THIS HOOK NO LONGER DOES, AND WHERE IT IS DONE INSTEAD (R1284) ──
#
# THREE GATES THAT COMPILE OR DOCUMENT THE WHOLE TREE LEFT THE COMMIT PATH:
# workspace clippy, `item-citations`, and the separate-workspace lint. Each is
# still run — none of them was dropped — and each is named below with where.
#
# WHY THEY LEFT, MEASURED RATHER THAN FELT. On 2026-08-23 this hook could not
# finish a single commit across eleven attempts: `item-citations` documents every
# target in the workspace, and the workstation was at load 17–35 with three
# sibling repositories building on it. A gate that cannot complete does not
# protect anything — it stops the round, and the pressure it puts on the author
# is pressure toward `--no-verify`, which is the one thing no exemption here
# covers. The 2026-08-20 note that used to sit under Gate 5 said the wrapper
# cannot place work it is never handed; the answer to that is not to hand a
# workstation more work, it is to stop doing the world's work per commit.
#
# WHERE EACH ONE IS NOW, and every one of these was checked rather than assumed:
#
#   workspace clippy    `.githooks/pre-push` (routed through `$BX` since
#                       `24b4fce`, so it lands on a build machine when one is
#                       configured). NOT hosted — this is the one that has no
#                       job of its own, and pre-push is therefore its only run.
#   item-citations      the hosted `item citations name items` job, which carries
#                       its own cache and its own budget for exactly this reason.
#   side workspaces     `.githooks/pre-push` runs the script WHOLE, and so does
#                       the hosted `separate in-repo workspaces` job. The
#                       `--lint-only` call here was the weakest of the three.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. A citation that names no item can
# now be committed and pushed; it is caught by the hosted run, and `ci-state`
# prints that run's verdict inside the NEXT push. So the window widened from
# "before this commit" to "before the push after this one" — the same trade this
# repository already made when it stopped blocking on hosted runs.
#
# WHAT STAYS HERE IS WHAT A COMMIT IS THE RIGHT MOMENT FOR: `cargo fmt --check`
# parses and does not compile; the vN ban and the citation defense are about the
# STAGED DIFF, which no later gate can see; and the syntax walks each build one
# small tool and read the tree.

# Gate 5a — item citations, the sibling `validate-code-refs` never had. That
# gate rejects a `Round NNN` in code that names no ledger entry; nothing rejected
# a `[`foo`]` that names no ITEM, and rustdoc — the resolver the toolchain
# already ships — had never been pointed at this workspace. R1077 pointed it and
# 34 links resolved to nothing, two of them advertising `mnemosyne-query`'s entry
# points by names the crate no longer had.
#
# ONE DEFINITION, in `tools/item-citations`, which is what CI runs. R1077's form
# here was `cargo doc --workspace`: it documents 25 of this workspace's 136
# targets, so a citation inside any of the 106 test targets — or inside a binary
# whose name collides with its package's library — passed it untouched. Both
# holes had a live citation in them when R1078 measured.
#
# The gate is resolved from THIS HOOK's location and pointed at the WORKING
# DIRECTORY, which are two different trees whenever this repository's hook runs
# over another one — its own smoke test does exactly that, and it is what caught
# the first version of this gate, which named the manifest relative to the cwd
# and so did not exist wherever it was actually invoked. R1069 made the same
# correction for the separate-workspace gate below; a gate that silently
# vanishes when its program is not under the caller's cwd is a gate that stops
# existing without saying so.
# ⇒ MOVED TO THE HOSTED `item citations name items` JOB (R1284). The reasoning
# above is why the gate exists and it has not changed; what changed is WHERE it
# runs. Documenting 136 targets is the most expensive thing this repository asks
# of any machine, the hosted job carries its own cache and budget for it, and on
# a contended workstation this call is what made eleven consecutive commits
# unable to finish. The three exit codes it distinguishes — clean, a citation
# naming no item, and could-not-read — are read there by the same tool.

# Gate 5c — in test code a wait ends on a condition and its budget is named.
#
# R1073 turned main red with `conflicts > 0` after running eight writers for
# 500ms: an assertion whose subject is how the scheduler interleaved, wearing
# the clothes of an assertion about the store. R1075 swept `bench` and found the
# same shape in a neighbouring crate under the same comment. NOBODY had asked
# the root workspace, which is the tree that runs on every push — and it held
# four waits that end on a clock, one of which (`zero_rate_sampling_drops_all_
# spans`) had no red side at all: it slept 300ms and then required that nothing
# had arrived, so a wait too SHORT passed it.
#
# The gate parses (`syn`) rather than searching for text, because the difference
# between a `sleep` that IS the synchronisation and a `sleep` that is the retry
# step of a poll is the entire law, and no pattern sees it. Measured at 9.5s
# warm on this workspace — cheaper than Gate 5a above.
#
# Resolved from THIS HOOK's location, pointed at the WORKING DIRECTORY, for the
# reason spelled out under Gate 5a.
if printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qE '\.rs$'; then
    # WHICH WORKSPACE OWNS EACH STAGED `.rs` (R1292), and why the four gates
    # below are pointed at THOSE rather than at the root.
    #
    # THE TRIGGER AND THE TARGET DID NOT AGREE. Each of these gates fires when
    # ANY `.rs` is staged and then walked `$(pwd)/Cargo.toml` — the root — while
    # this repository has TWENTY-SIX workspace roots. Staging a file under
    # `tools/<crate>/` therefore ran four whole-workspace walks over a tree that
    # does not contain it, and every one of them reported findings about files
    # the commit never touched and none about the file it did. Zero findings is
    # what a clean tree looks like.
    #
    # MEASURED, BY IT HAPPENING. R1291 added `tools/injection-harness/tests/
    # anchors.rs`, whose cases spawn a binary that reads `CARGO` without naming
    # it. `named-environment` is exactly the gate for that, it RAN on that
    # commit, and it ran over the root — so the commit passed, the push passed,
    # and the hosted `separate in-repo workspaces` job found it one round later.
    # Nothing was broken; the gate was pointed at the wrong tree.
    #
    # WHY IT IS NOT A VENUE QUESTION. The owner's instruction is that gates
    # which compile or document the WHOLE tree belong to CI, and R1284 moved the
    # separate-workspace LINT gate off this path for that reason. These four
    # compile nothing: they parse `.rs` with `syn`, and one of them over one side
    # workspace is 2.6s. What changes here is not how much is checked but WHICH
    # TREE — the set is the workspaces this commit's own files live in, so a
    # root-only commit pays exactly what it paid before.
    #
    # THE FIRST `[workspace]` ABOVE A FILE OWNS IT, walked from the file rather
    # than matched against a list, so a workspace added to this repository joins
    # this gate the day its manifest does. A file with no `[workspace]` above it
    # is REFUSED rather than gated by the root: a file no workspace owns is a
    # file no walk below reads, and that is the state this block exists to end.
    tree_root="$(pwd)"
    rs_workspaces=()
    while IFS= read -r rs_file; do
        [[ "$rs_file" == *.rs ]] || continue
        if ! rs_dir="$(cd "$(dirname "$rs_file")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)"; then
            {
                echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the directory of $rs_file"
                echo "  cannot be entered, so which workspace gates it is unanswerable"
            } >&2
            exit 1
        fi
        while :; do
            rs_manifest="$rs_dir/Cargo.toml"
            if [[ -f "$rs_manifest" ]]; then
                # A MANIFEST THAT EXISTS AND CANNOT BE READ IS NOT ONE WITHOUT A
                # `[workspace]`. Walking past it would hand this file to whatever
                # workspace lies further up, which is the mispointing above with
                # an extra step in front of it.
                if [[ ! -r "$rs_manifest" ]]; then
                    {
                        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — $rs_manifest exists and"
                        echo "  cannot be read, so which workspace gates $rs_file is unanswerable"
                    } >&2
                    exit 1
                fi
                if grep -q '^\[workspace\]' "$rs_manifest"; then
                    case " ${rs_workspaces[*]-} " in
                    *" $rs_manifest "*) ;;
                    *) rs_workspaces+=("$rs_manifest") ;;
                    esac
                    break
                fi
            fi
            if [[ "$rs_dir" == "$tree_root" || "$rs_dir" == "/" ]]; then
                {
                    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — no [workspace] above"
                    echo "  $rs_file up to $tree_root, so the four gates below would read"
                    echo "  a tree this file is not in and report nothing about it"
                } >&2
                exit 1
            fi
            rs_dir="$(dirname "$rs_dir")"
        done
    done <<< "$staged"
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] staged .rs live in ${#rs_workspaces[@]} workspace(s):" \
         "${rs_workspaces[*]}" >&2

    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — waits end on conditions" >&2
    # The checkout, not the manifest — see Gate 5b.
    gates="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
    if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/blind-waits/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the blind-wait gate is" \
             "missing at $gates/tools/blind-waits/Cargo.toml" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    # THE TWO NON-ZERO EXITS MEAN DIFFERENT THINGS and printing one message for
    # both is how a gate teaches people the wrong lesson: 1 is "these sites
    # break the law", 2 is "I could not read enough of this tree to have an
    # opinion". The second one already misreported once, when a workspace
    # holding no test code was an exit 2 and this line called it a wait on a
    # clock.
    # `|| waits_verdict=$?` rather than a bare call and `$?`: this file runs
    # under `set -e`, which kills it at the failing command before any exit-code
    # branch can read one. The first version of this block did exactly that, and
    # the hook's own smoke test caught it — the gate's message reached stderr
    # and the hook's did not.
    # ONE RUN PER WORKSPACE THIS COMMIT'S OWN `.rs` LIVE IN (R1292), and the loop
    # stops at the first verdict that is not 0 so the message below is about the
    # tree that produced it.
    waits_verdict=0
    for rs_manifest in "${rs_workspaces[@]}"; do
        cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/blind-waits/Cargo.toml" \
            --bin blind-waits -- --workspace "$rs_manifest" || waits_verdict=$?
        [[ $waits_verdict -eq 0 ]] || break
    done
    if [[ $waits_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — a test waits on a clock"
            echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/blind-waits/Cargo.toml \\"
            echo "         --bin blind-waits -- --workspace Cargo.toml"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    elif [[ $waits_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the blind-wait gate could" \
                 "not read this tree (exit $waits_verdict); its own message is above"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # Gate 5d — every environment a spawned program reads is one its test names
    # (R1182). Same three-code contract as the gate above, and here for the same
    # reason: R1181 turned `main` red with a fixture whose environment list had
    # gone stale, green on every developer's machine and red on the runner.
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — a test names what it spawns" >&2
    if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/named-environment/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the named-environment gate" \
             "is missing at $gates/tools/named-environment/Cargo.toml" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    environment_verdict=0
    for rs_manifest in "${rs_workspaces[@]}"; do
        cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/named-environment/Cargo.toml" \
            --bin named-environment -- --workspace "$rs_manifest" || environment_verdict=$?
        [[ $environment_verdict -eq 0 ]] || break
    done
    if [[ $environment_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — a test leaves a variable" \
                 "its spawned program reads to the machine"
            echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/named-environment/Cargo.toml \\"
            echo "         --bin named-environment -- --workspace Cargo.toml"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    elif [[ $environment_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the named-environment gate" \
                 "could not read this tree (exit $environment_verdict); its own message is above"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # Gate 5f — a path built from the shared temp root names the process
    # (R1193). Same three-code contract as the two gates above.
    #
    # R1175 repaired six fixture paths that composed `std::env::temp_dir()` with
    # a name fixed in the source, after two overlapping runs of
    # `check-side-workspaces.sh` made one delete the other's directory mid-write.
    # Its carry said what it could not do in four words — NOTHING STOPS THE
    # SEVENTH ONE — and discipline is not a mechanism.
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — a scratch path names its owner" >&2
    if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/unowned-scratch/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the scratch-ownership gate" \
             "is missing at $gates/tools/unowned-scratch/Cargo.toml" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    scratch_verdict=0
    for rs_manifest in "${rs_workspaces[@]}"; do
        cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/unowned-scratch/Cargo.toml" \
            --bin unowned-scratch -- --workspace "$rs_manifest" || scratch_verdict=$?
        [[ $scratch_verdict -eq 0 ]] || break
    done
    if [[ $scratch_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — a path under the shared temp" \
                 "root names no owner, so two runs share it"
            echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/unowned-scratch/Cargo.toml \\"
            echo "         --bin unowned-scratch -- --workspace Cargo.toml"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    elif [[ $scratch_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the scratch-ownership gate" \
                 "could not read this tree (exit $scratch_verdict); its own message is above"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # Gate 5g — nothing creates an executable file (R1192). Same three-code
    # contract as the three gates above.
    #
    # `exec` refuses a file some process holds open for writing with `ETXTBSY`,
    # and the holder is a SIBLING test's fork inheriting the descriptor rather
    # than the thread that wrote it. R1192 met it in `unread-declaration`, whose
    # eleven cases each wrote a shell script and ran it: green alone, in every
    # run, until eleven ran beside ten other crates under
    # `check-side-workspaces.sh` and one died. That round repaired one crate and
    # wrote down what it could not do — NOTHING ASKS THE OTHERS — and ten more
    # sites were sitting in seven workspaces.
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — nothing writes a program it runs" >&2
    if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/written-executable/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the written-executable gate" \
             "is missing at $gates/tools/written-executable/Cargo.toml" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    executable_verdict=0
    for rs_manifest in "${rs_workspaces[@]}"; do
        cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/written-executable/Cargo.toml" \
            --bin written-executable -- --workspace "$rs_manifest" || executable_verdict=$?
        [[ $executable_verdict -eq 0 ]] || break
    done
    if [[ $executable_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — a file this repository" \
                 "writes is one it then runs"
            echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/written-executable/Cargo.toml \\"
            echo "         --bin written-executable -- --workspace Cargo.toml"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    elif [[ $executable_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the written-executable gate" \
                 "could not read this tree (exit $executable_verdict); its own message is above"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi
fi

# Gate 5b — separate in-repo workspaces (own `[workspace]`; Gates 4/5 above,
# `--all`/`--workspace`, never reach them). Structural: for each staged `.rs`,
# walk up to its owning `[workspace]` root and gate any non-root one on its own
# manifest. Runs only when such a file is staged, so the root gate stays fast.
# ⇒ MOVED OFF THE COMMIT PATH (R1284), and this one was already the weakest of
# the three: it asked for the LINT HALF of a script that `.githooks/pre-push`
# and the hosted `separate in-repo workspaces` job each run WHOLE. Both of those
# run fmt, clippy, the syntax walks AND the suites, over every separate
# workspace rather than over the ones a commit happens to touch — so nothing
# this call could find is outside what already follows it, and what it cost was
# a clippy compile of a whole side workspace per commit.

# Gate 5e — every key this repository declares to the build-machine program is
# one that program reads (R1189).
#
# NO `if staged` GUARD, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The other gates here run when a
# file of theirs is staged because their subject is in this tree. This one's
# subject is HALF outside it: the declaration is here and its only reader is
# `~/.claude/remote-build/bin/bx`, a machine-wide program that can rename a key
# or change a pattern without a single byte moving in this checkout. A trigger
# that waited for `.claude/remote-build.toml` to be staged would never fire for
# the half that actually rots.
#
# It has rotted twice on this one file. `packages` was documented for as long as
# the format was and four repositories declared it, and nothing read it until a
# new build machine failed a compile for a missing header. `peak_gb_per_task`
# held a measurement of a defect that had already been fixed, and the cost was
# not slowness but a fleet: a 21GB host `not a candidate` and a 125GB one held to
# four threads with 28 cores idle (R1188). The measurement that opened this gate
# found a third — `exclude`, declared by ALL FIVE repositories on this machine
# and extracted by nothing, one of them (`scxml-core-engine`'s `/embed`) tracked
# and therefore sent on every transfer by a repository that declared it never
# would be.
#
# The three codes, as Gates 5a/5c/5d read them. 2 here means the program could
# not be asked — a repository that declares nothing is exit 0 with a sentence,
# not a refusal, which is what the hook's own smoke test runs over.
#
# Measured at 1.8s warm, which is what an unconditional gate has to be worth:
# the program is asked once, reads one file, and runs nothing.
echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] the build-machine declaration is read as declared" >&2
gates="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/unread-declaration/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the declaration gate is missing" \
         "at $gates/tools/unread-declaration/Cargo.toml" >&2
    exit 1
fi
declaration_verdict=0
cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/unread-declaration/Cargo.toml" \
    --bin unread-declaration -- --repo "$(pwd)" || declaration_verdict=$?
if [[ $declaration_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — a declared key is not read as declared"
        echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/unread-declaration/Cargo.toml \\"
        echo "         --bin unread-declaration -- --repo ."
    } >&2
    exit 1
elif [[ $declaration_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the declaration gate could not ask" \
             "the program that reads it (exit $declaration_verdict); its own message is above"
    } >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Gate 5h — every package CI installs is one the build-machine declaration
# names (R1210).
#
# THE FAR SIDE THAT SPELLS ITSELF OUT IS THE WITNESS FOR THE ONE THAT DOES NOT.
# "Everything a build machine needs" has no answer without a baseline — it also
# needs a shell and a linker — and CI is the only far side this repository
# specifies completely: a stock runner image, plus every departure written out as
# a step. So what CI installs is exactly the set already discovered to be missing
# from a bare machine, and `.claude/remote-build.toml` is where that set belongs.
#
# It was not there. `protobuf-compiler` has been installed by these workflows
# since 2026-07-31 (R888, which noted that the local hooks could not see the
# defect because this machine HAS protoc); the declaration was written on
# 2026-08-12 naming `libclang-common-18-dev` alone, and the gap surfaced on
# 2026-08-14 the only way it could — a host without it was chosen and 269 crates
# compiled before the build script failed. Run against that history, this gate is
# red at the commit that created the declaration and green at the commit that
# repaired it, two days later.
#
# NO `if staged` GUARD, for a different reason than Gate 5e's: both halves are in
# this tree, and the trigger would be right. It is measured at 0.05s warm, which
# is cheaper than deciding whether to run it.
#
# The three codes, as Gates 5a/5c/5d/5e read them. 2 here means the population or
# the declaration could not be read — a workflow that installs through an action,
# a manager this law does not read, or a tree with nothing to compare.
echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] what CI installs is what the build machine is told" >&2
gates="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/undeclared-requirement/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the requirement gate is missing" \
         "at $gates/tools/undeclared-requirement/Cargo.toml" >&2
    exit 1
fi
requirement_verdict=0
cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/undeclared-requirement/Cargo.toml" \
    --bin undeclared-requirement -- --repo "$(pwd)" || requirement_verdict=$?
if [[ $requirement_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — CI installs something the build-machine" \
             "declaration does not name"
        echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/undeclared-requirement/Cargo.toml \\"
        echo "         --bin undeclared-requirement -- --repo ."
    } >&2
    exit 1
elif [[ $requirement_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the requirement gate could not judge" \
             "what CI installs (exit $requirement_verdict); its own message is above"
    } >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Gate 5i — anchor gate (R1291) — `tools/injection-harness anchors`, on EVERY
# commit: which of this repository's own sweeps THE CHANGE BEING COMMITTED
# breaks. An injection's anchor is exact text and the source it names moves, so
# the round that moves a gate is the one holding the information — and nothing
# asked it. R1287 took one anchor with a block it removed and orphaned another
# injection's firing record; a hosted job found both ONE ROUND LATER, and R1289
# had to `git log -S` the anchor text to learn which commit had done it.
#
# THE INDEX AGAINST `HEAD`, which is what makes it safe here. Reporting every
# dead anchor in the tree would stop a commit for damage its author never
# touched, and this working tree has more than one writer. Standing findings are
# PRINTED and never blocking; the difference is what this commit owes.
#
# NO `if staged` GUARD: a change anywhere in the tree can move an anchor's text,
# and the population is every tracked sweep rather than a staged path. Measured
# at 1.8s warm over 38 sweeps and 426 tracked `.json`, which is the cost of one
# `git cat-file --batch` per revision — a `git show` per file is 1.83s of spawn
# alone, and that measurement is why the reader is batched.
#
# The three codes, as Gates 5a/5c/5d/5e/5h read them. 2 here means git could not
# be asked, which is not the same as a tree whose anchors hold.
echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] what this change breaks in this repository's own sweeps" >&2
gates="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
if [[ ! -f "$gates/tools/injection-harness/Cargo.toml" ]]; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the anchor gate is missing" \
         "at $gates/tools/injection-harness/Cargo.toml" >&2
    exit 1
fi
anchor_verdict=0
cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/injection-harness/Cargo.toml" \
    -- anchors --repo "$(pwd)" || anchor_verdict=$?
if [[ $anchor_verdict -eq 1 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — this change breaks a sweep of this"
        echo "  repository's own; the two repairs are named above, and they are not the same"
        echo "  run: cargo run -q --manifest-path tools/injection-harness/Cargo.toml \\"
        echo "         -- anchors --repo ."
    } >&2
    exit 1
elif [[ $anchor_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
    {
        echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the anchor gate could not be asked" \
             "about this change (exit $anchor_verdict); its own message is above"
    } >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Gate 6 — vN version-postfix identifier ban (CLAUDE.md anti-pattern).
# Extending a definition must modify it in place; no `foo_v2` / `BarV3`
# version postfixes anywhere — production OR test fixtures/names/data.
# Scans ADDED lines in staged `.rs` files. `_v[0-9]` intentionally does
# not match `_version` (schema_version etc. are real version numbers).
if printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qE '\.rs$'; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] .rs staged — vN version-postfix ban" >&2
    # THE READ AND THE SEARCH ARE SEPARATED, AND R1283 IS WHY (R1281 fixed the
    # same defect nine lines up and did not look at this one). `grep` answers 1
    # when it finds NOTHING, which is the ordinary case here, so a `|| true` is
    # needed — and under `set -o pipefail` that same clause also swallowed a
    # FAILED `git diff --cached`, leaving `vn_hits` empty and the gate passing.
    # One clause cannot tell "no hits" from "could not look", so the read is
    # checked on its own and only the search is allowed to come back empty.
    if ! added=$(git diff --cached --unified=0 --diff-filter=ACM -- '*.rs'); then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — the staged diff could not be read"
            echo "  the vN ban is a search over it, and an unreadable diff is not an empty one"
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    vn_hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$added" \
        | grep -E '^\+' | grep -vE '^\+\+\+' \
        | grep -E '[A-Za-z0-9]_v[0-9]|[a-z0-9]V[0-9]' || true)
    if [[ -n "$vn_hits" ]]; then
        {
            echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] commit rejected — vN version-postfix identifier"
            echo "  banned: foo_v2 / BarV3 version postfixes — extend the definition in place."
            echo "  offending added lines:"
            printf '%s\n' "$vn_hits" | sed 's/^/    /'
        } >&2
        exit 1
    fi
fi

# Gate 7 — put this tree's census on a machine that is not this one (R1234).
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT AT PUSH. `tools/outside-reach` answers what this
# repository's suite reaches outside itself, and that answer is a claim about a
# MACHINE. It has been wrong four times and every one was found somewhere its
# author was not sitting — a hosted runner's HOME, a gcc driver's spelling, a
# `find(1)` reaching a descriptor through `fcntl` in a population no workstation
# run reproduces. There was no automated second machine until this gate: the
# hosted run happens once per push and after it, and the two build-machine arms
# that found the last two defects were launched by hand.
#
# A COMMIT IS WHEN THE BYTES OF A ROUND STOP MOVING, which is why the dispatch is
# here and the reading is at push. The census takes minutes on the machine it is
# sent to; those minutes are spent while this round writes its ledger entry and
# runs the push's own gates, so the lane costs no wall clock. The fingerprint it
# claims is over TRACKED CONTENT and not over `HEAD`, precisely so that the
# commit this hook is part of does not turn the answer into one about another
# tree.
#
# IT REPORTS AND NEVER BLOCKS, the semantics `pre-push`'s CI reporter has and for
# the same reason recorded there: a machine being busy, absent or mid-upgrade is
# not this repository's defect, and a commit that cannot be made while the fleet
# is down is a gate that stops the work it exists to check. The ANSWER is not
# softened by that — `one-machine --read` still exits 0, 1 or 2 honestly for
# every caller that wants to gate on it.
#
# NO `-f` GUARD IN FRONT OF THE PROGRAM, unlike the gates above and for the
# reason `pre-push`'s CI reporter states: it is resolved from THIS checkout,
# where it always exists, so the guard is a branch nothing could reach — and one
# that is missing makes `cargo run` fail, which is the branch below, and that one
# prints the true sentence. `gates` is re-derived rather than inherited from the
# block above it, because a gate whose path comes from a neighbour is a gate that
# breaks when the neighbour moves.
echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] the census of this tree, on a machine that is not this one" >&2
gates="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
census_verdict=0
cargo run -q --locked --manifest-path "$gates/tools/one-machine/Cargo.toml" \
    --bin one-machine -- --repo "$(pwd)" --send || census_verdict=$?
if [[ $census_verdict -ne 0 ]]; then
    echo "[mnemosyne pre-commit] NOTE no second machine has this tree" \
         "(exit $census_verdict); its own message is above. The push will say so too" >&2
fi
